{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AiH7Gqx1pdWw24Aa2jKCdp","type":"tool_result","content":" .../WI-579-the-verdict-carrier-and-the-ad.md       | 304 ---------------------\n ...-579-verdict-carrier-and-adjudication-review.md |  15 +\n docs/requirements/interfaces.toml                  |   2 +-\n docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml      |   4 +-\n 4 files changed, 18 insertions(+), 307 deletions(-)\n=== untracked active ===\ntotal 40\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 3 diytechy  staff     96 Sep  3 03:11 .\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 4 diytechy  staff    128 Sep  2 20:21 ..\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 diytechy  staff  20041 Sep  3 03:11 WI-579-the-verdict-carrier-and-the-ad.md\n=== iteration log 026 ===\ndiff --git a/docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml b/docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml\nindex 3cb57eff96f8c140a14cb4ee5397f9dcccc95af9..3b7a1e9918749eb9b4d6c509ab0893d04ee8336a\n--- a/docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml\n+++ b/docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml\n@@ -2184,8 +2184,8 @@\n title = \"The verdict record: what carries a governing verdict, and what binds it to the tree it judged\"\n module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/verdict.py\"\n code_symbol = \"RECORD_PREFIXES/fold_listing/tree_identity/refresh_subject/refresh_attestation/work_tip/governing_rev/governing_identity/format_trailer/parse_trailer/round_file/session_log/branch_paths/logged_rounds/round_entries/branch_entries/declared_phases/phases_owed/round_count/format_branch_trailer/branch_trailers\"\n-detail = \"\"\"The reading behind the merge seam's verdict rung, sited below both of its readers. tree_identity folds `git ls-tree -r -z` with the RECORD prefixes dropped - docs/reviews/, docs/log.d/, docs/iteration/ - into a SHA-256 over the surviving entries, which is what makes \"the tree a verdict judged\" a value two independent readers can compare; the digest is 64 hex deliberately, so it cannot be mistaken for the git tree object id the Bar-Green trailer carries. -z is the ENCODING BOUNDARY and not a formatting choice: git quotes any path holding a non-ASCII or special character, and a quoted path defeats the record-prefix test, so fold_listing takes raw byte entries and never decodes them or sees git's display encoding. What CANNOT invalidate a verdict has two halves and this module owns both: the record PATHS, and the station's refresh COMMIT. refresh_attestation verifies a Bar-Green: tree=<40 hex> work=<40 hex> trailer against the commit's own tree, its first parent and this branch's refresh subject; the peel is then written TWICE, on purpose, because its two callers can afford different answers: work_tip peels only a refresh sitting on the tip, at the work sha the refresh itself recorded, because it feeds a reset --hard where peeling one commit too far destroys committed work; governing_rev walks THROUGH any commit whose non-record identity equals its first parent's, to reach a refresh it would otherwise hide, which a read-only reader can do safely. Merging the two is a defect in either direction, and leaving them merged was one: while the identity used the tip-only peel, a single telemetry commit - a record path the fold exists to ignore - moved the identity by burying the refresh under it, so an approval served before the refresh named a tree nothing governed and both readers agreed on the wrong answer. The walk's step condition is that sentence itself - a commit that cannot invalidate a verdict - and not a proxy for it, because the proxy was the same defect one shape further on: a predicate CLASSIFYING the paths a commit touched must stop at every commit whose paths it cannot read, and an attestation riding unchanged bookkeeping commits EMPTY, so the commit RECORDING an approval buried the refresh. Asking the identity question directly makes the empty carrier, the merge commit and the quoted-path trap unrepresentable together, since no path is classified in the walk at all. governing_identity composes governing_rev with the fold, so the merge slot and the loop's review-owed derivation are HANDED one answer rather than each choosing the rev it measures at; it takes the REV as an optional argument, because the readers that ask about a COMMIT rather than about the branch - the sha a round file cites, and the commit an attestation rides - must be answered by the same composition, and a raw fold there is that composition with the peel left out. Leaving it out was the same defect once more: a round drawn AFTER a refresh names the post-refresh tree while the gate governs by the peeled one, so the two were permanently unequal and no commit on the branch could make them agree - the round invisible to both readers, and an identical one re-drawn every tick. fold_listing is pure over the entries, so the one rule that decides whether two trees are the same work is exercisable without a repository. logged_rounds is the provenance join: a file under docs/reviews/ is a ROUND only when the coordinator's own committed session log for that (train, ordinal) declares a REVIEW phase, so an implementer-authored file in the review path is not evidence. round_entries restricts the rounds to those whose reviewed commit GOVERNS the identity under judgement - through governing_identity at that commit, never the bare fold - and hands the phase/ordinal/verdict triples to the latest-per-phase rule the scoring layer already owned; the parse function is INJECTED rather than imported, so th
